1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,009
Here is a game you know. Tic-tac-toe. Three in a row, in a column, or along a diagonal

2
00:00:07,009 --> 00:00:07,416
wins.

3
00:00:07,800 --> 00:00:14,952
Now change the game. No turns. You colour every cell of the board at once, red or blue.

4
00:00:15,333 --> 00:00:21,741
Red wins with a whole red line. Blue has it easier: blue wins with just two blue cells

5
00:00:21,741 --> 00:00:23,901
next to each other on a line.

6
00:00:24,300 --> 00:00:27,485
Can you colour the board so that nobody wins?

7
00:00:27,485 --> 00:00:31,308
And what happens on bigger boards, in more dimensions?

8
00:00:31,700 --> 00:00:38,665
First, what exactly is a line? Name each cell by its column, then its row, counting from

9
00:00:38,665 --> 00:00:42,860
zero. This corner is zero zero. This cell is two one.

10
00:00:43,233 --> 00:00:50,815
A line is a pattern with a star. Star one means: replace the star by zero, then one,

11
00:00:50,815 --> 00:00:55,148
then two. That gives zero one, one one, two one.

12
00:00:55,148 --> 00:00:55,689
A row.

13
00:00:56,067 --> 00:01:04,265
One star gives a column. Star star gives the diagonal: zero zero, one one, two two.

14
00:01:04,265 --> 00:01:08,019
Every star always gets the same digit.

15
00:01:08,400 --> 00:01:12,358
The other diagonal goes zero two, one one, two zero.

16
00:01:12,358 --> 00:01:17,535
One digit goes up while the other goes down, so no pattern makes it.

17
00:01:17,535 --> 00:01:20,808
Here, it is not a line. Seven lines in all.

18
00:01:21,200 --> 00:01:27,554
Two cells are neighbours on a line when their stars hold consecutive digits, like zero

19
00:01:27,554 --> 00:01:32,504
and one, or one and two. The two ends of a line are not neighbours.

20
00:01:32,900 --> 00:01:39,204
So here is the puzzle. Every one of the seven lines needs a blue cell, but no line may

21
00:01:39,204 --> 00:01:42,356
have two blue neighbours. Pause and try it.

22
00:01:43,933 --> 00:01:48,194
A natural first idea: add up the two digits of each cell.

23
00:01:48,194 --> 00:01:53,053
One step along a row or a column changes that sum by exactly one.

24
00:01:53,433 --> 00:01:56,917
So colour the odd sums blue, like a chessboard.

25
00:01:56,917 --> 00:02:02,625
Two odd numbers are never one apart, so no row or column has blue neighbours.

26
00:02:02,625 --> 00:02:05,145
And each has an odd sum somewhere.

27
00:02:05,533 --> 00:02:09,865
But the diagonal has sums zero, two and four.

28
00:02:09,865 --> 00:02:14,293
All even. The whole diagonal is red. Red wins.

29
00:02:14,667 --> 00:02:20,136
How could you rescue the diagonal without breaking anything else?

30
00:02:20,136 --> 00:02:21,483
Pause and think.

31
00:02:23,067 --> 00:02:28,672
Try making the corner zero zero blue. The diagonal is saved.

32
00:02:28,672 --> 00:02:36,052
But zero zero now sits next to one zero in its row, and zero one in its column.

33
00:02:36,052 --> 00:02:36,987
Blue wins.

34
00:02:37,367 --> 00:02:41,975
So turn those two red. Did that leave any line without blue?

35
00:02:43,167 --> 00:02:46,864
The bottom row and the left column now have zero zero.

36
00:02:46,864 --> 00:02:51,519
The middle row still has two one, and the middle column has one two.

37
00:02:51,519 --> 00:02:55,695
Every line has a blue cell, and there are no blue neighbours.

38
00:02:55,695 --> 00:02:56,175
A draw!

39
00:02:56,567 --> 00:03:00,475
One two and two one touch at a corner, but share no line.

40
00:03:00,475 --> 00:03:05,207
That's fine. And notice: the middle row was rescued by its last cell.

41
00:03:05,207 --> 00:03:06,167
Remember that.

42
00:03:06,567 --> 00:03:12,674
Now add a dimension. Cells get three digits, and the board becomes a three by three by

43
00:03:12,674 --> 00:03:15,231
three cube, with thirty seven lines.

44
00:03:15,600 --> 00:03:20,293
Here, a draw is impossible. However you colour the cube, someone wins.

45
00:03:20,293 --> 00:03:23,712
A computer search over every colouring confirms it.

46
00:03:24,100 --> 00:03:28,963
In general, give each cell n digits, each from zero up to k minus one.

47
00:03:28,963 --> 00:03:33,964
That is n dimensions. What is the smallest n where a draw is impossible?

48
00:03:34,333 --> 00:03:37,254
That number is written H J of k, two, two.

49
00:03:37,254 --> 00:03:42,608
Red needs a whole line of k cells, blue needs two, and there are two colours.

50
00:03:42,608 --> 00:03:45,181
For tic-tac-toe, the answer is three.

51
00:03:45,567 --> 00:03:51,491
In 1963, Alfred Hales and Robert Jewett proved that in the classic version, where both

52
00:03:51,491 --> 00:03:56,175
colours need a whole line, enough dimensions always rule out a draw.

53
00:03:56,567 --> 00:04:02,150
In 2026, Conlon, Farnsworth and Robertson studied this lopsided version.

54
00:04:02,150 --> 00:04:06,183
They proved that dimension k always forces a winner.

55
00:04:06,183 --> 00:04:07,967
That is an upper bound.

56
00:04:08,333 --> 00:04:14,142
They showed the answer is exactly k when k is prime, and guessed that it is always k.

57
00:04:14,142 --> 00:04:17,081
Computer searches confirmed it up to seven.

58
00:04:17,081 --> 00:04:19,541
Eight was left open: seven or eight.

59
00:04:19,933 --> 00:04:24,118
The new result settles eight, and infinitely many more.

60
00:04:24,118 --> 00:04:29,749
For every prime p, when k is p plus one, the answer is exactly p plus one.

61
00:04:30,133 --> 00:04:33,017
Seven gives eight, and eleven gives twelve.

62
00:04:33,017 --> 00:04:38,582
Since dimension p plus one forces a winner, the real work is a draw in dimension p.

63
00:04:38,582 --> 00:04:41,197
For p equals two: our tic-tac-toe draw.

64
00:04:41,567 --> 00:04:44,293
For any prime p, here is the colouring.

65
00:04:44,293 --> 00:04:49,395
Add up a cell's digits. Blue if the sum is one more than a multiple of p.

66
00:04:49,395 --> 00:04:55,127
Then our repair: add the zero cell, and remove the cells whose sum is exactly one.

67
00:04:55,500 --> 00:05:00,129
For p equals two, the blue sums are zero and three: our board.

68
00:05:00,129 --> 00:05:06,252
For p equals three, on a four by four by four cube, they are zero, four and seven.

69
00:05:06,633 --> 00:05:11,534
Why does this work for every prime? Watch the sums along a line.

70
00:05:11,534 --> 00:05:16,970
Our middle row goes one, two, three. The diagonal goes zero, two, four.

71
00:05:16,970 --> 00:05:17,889
Equal steps.

72
00:05:18,267 --> 00:05:23,365
In general, take a pattern with d stars, whose fixed digits add up to S.

73
00:05:23,365 --> 00:05:26,197
Filling the stars with a adds d times a.

74
00:05:26,197 --> 00:05:31,083
So the sums go S, S plus d, S plus two d, and so on: equal hops of d.

75
00:05:31,467 --> 00:05:36,852
Here is a line from the seven dimensional case: three stars, and fixed digits adding

76
00:05:36,852 --> 00:05:40,635
to five. Its sums hop by three, from five up to twenty six.

77
00:05:41,033 --> 00:05:47,065
So we need two things. The hops must land on blue at least once, and never on blue twice

78
00:05:47,065 --> 00:05:47,681
in a row.

79
00:05:48,067 --> 00:05:51,372
Second job first. Say d is less than p.

80
00:05:51,372 --> 00:05:55,270
Blue sums above one are a multiple of p apart.

81
00:05:55,270 --> 00:05:59,762
Neighbours are d apart, and d is not a multiple of p.

82
00:05:59,762 --> 00:06:01,627
So no blue neighbours.

83
00:06:02,000 --> 00:06:06,659
The zero cell always starts its line, and the next sum is d.

84
00:06:06,659 --> 00:06:12,172
If d is one, that cell was removed. Otherwise its remainder is not one.

85
00:06:12,172 --> 00:06:12,560
Safe.

86
00:06:12,933 --> 00:06:20,405
If every digit is a star, then d is p, and the sums are zero, p, two p, and so on.

87
00:06:20,405 --> 00:06:24,141
Only zero is blue. That was our diagonal.

88
00:06:24,533 --> 00:06:29,526
Now the first job. Picture remainders on a clock with p hours.

89
00:06:29,526 --> 00:06:36,533
Each hop turns the hand d hours. On a seven hour clock, hops of three visit every hour.

90
00:06:36,933 --> 00:06:41,071
Any hop from one to six works, because seven is prime.

91
00:06:41,071 --> 00:06:47,738
On a six hour clock, hops of two from zero visit only three hours, and never reach one.

92
00:06:47,738 --> 00:06:50,037
That is where we need a prime.

93
00:06:50,433 --> 00:06:55,460
So among the first p cells of any line, one has remainder one.

94
00:06:55,460 --> 00:07:01,704
Usually it is blue. The snag: its sum might be exactly one, which we removed.

95
00:07:01,704 --> 00:07:04,137
That happens in only two ways.

96
00:07:04,533 --> 00:07:07,935
Either it is the first cell, and S is one.

97
00:07:07,935 --> 00:07:11,985
Then jump to the last cell, where every star is p.

98
00:07:11,985 --> 00:07:18,141
Its sum, one plus d times p, is blue. That is why we need the extra digit p.

99
00:07:18,533 --> 00:07:24,509
Or it is a later cell. Then S must be zero, so the line starts at the zero cell, which

100
00:07:24,509 --> 00:07:27,845
is blue. Either way, every line has a blue cell.

101
00:07:28,233 --> 00:07:34,412
So in dimension p, nobody wins. Smaller boards sit inside this one, padded with zeros,

102
00:07:34,412 --> 00:07:40,089
so they have draws too. With the upper bound, the answer is exactly p plus one.

103
00:07:40,466 --> 00:07:44,207
Now the smallest new case: p is seven and k is eight.

104
00:07:44,207 --> 00:07:50,278
Seven digits, each from zero to seven: just over two million cells, or over thirty two

105
00:07:50,278 --> 00:07:52,466
thousand eight by eight boards.

106
00:07:52,866 --> 00:07:55,641
Here is how many cells have each digit sum.

107
00:07:55,641 --> 00:08:01,126
The blue sums are zero, eight, fifteen, twenty two, twenty nine, thirty six and forty

108
00:08:01,126 --> 00:08:04,482
three: just under three hundred thousand blue cells.

109
00:08:04,866 --> 00:08:10,403
A separate program checked every line, nearly two point seven million of them.

110
00:08:10,403 --> 00:08:15,018
Zero red lines. Zero blue neighbours. A draw in seven dimensions.

111
00:08:15,399 --> 00:08:19,720
That check covers this one board. The proof covers every prime at once.

112
00:08:19,720 --> 00:08:23,919
With the upper bound, the answer for k equals eight is exactly eight.

113
00:08:24,299 --> 00:08:29,726
The whole argument was also written in Lean, a language in which a computer checks every

114
00:08:29,726 --> 00:08:32,747
step of a proof against the basic rules of logic.

115
00:08:33,133 --> 00:08:39,277
Lean confirms the colouring works for every prime, re-proves the upper bound, and concludes

116
00:08:39,277 --> 00:08:44,341
the answer is p plus one, including eight and twelve, with no step assumed.

117
00:08:44,733 --> 00:08:48,906
Lean cannot check that its definitions match the question we care about.

118
00:08:48,906 --> 00:08:51,630
A separate review compared them with the paper.

119
00:08:51,630 --> 00:08:56,672
Nor can it say the result is new. A search of published work found nothing earlier, and

120
00:08:56,672 --> 00:08:58,701
the paper is not yet peer reviewed.

121
00:08:59,099 --> 00:09:04,190
To recap. One: blue must touch every line, but never twice in a row.

122
00:09:04,190 --> 00:09:10,478
Two: digit sums, plus a repair at zero and one, give a draw in dimension p for every

123
00:09:10,478 --> 00:09:15,419
prime p. Three: so when k is p plus one, the answer is p plus one.

124
00:09:15,799 --> 00:09:21,178
Still unknown: k equals nine and ten. For nine, the answer is eight or nine.

125
00:09:21,178 --> 00:09:24,929
For ten, eight, nine or ten. The guess says always k.

126
00:09:24,929 --> 00:09:31,015
That needs a draw in dimension k minus one, and nobody has found one, or ruled it out.

127
00:09:31,399 --> 00:09:36,655
The paper, the checking program and the Lean proof are all in the folder shown here.
